          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does protein powder raise blood sugar?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pure protein powders have a small direct effect on blood glucose compared with carbohydrate, because they contain little or no sugar or starch. Protein does stimulate some insulin, and flavored powders with maltodextrin or sugar can raise glucose more. Check total carbohydrate on the label and test your glucose two hours after a shake to see your own response."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is whey protein good for type 2 diabetes?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Small short-term trials suggest that 15 to 50 g of whey taken shortly before a meal can lower the glucose rise after that meal, likely by boosting insulin and gut hormones such as GLP-1. The studies are small and mostly excluded insulin users, so treat it as a promising habit to discuss with your care team, not a treatment."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a good diabetic protein shake to buy ready-made?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Look for about 20 to 30 g of protein with 1 to 2 g of sugar and single-digit carbohydrate per bottle. BOOST Glucose Control Max and Premier Protein both fit those numbers, per their labels. Meal-style diabetes shakes like Glucerna Hunger Smart carry less protein and more carbohydrate and fiber, so they suit a light meal rather than a protein top-up."
          }
