        <div id="read-the-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Read a Protein Label With Diabetes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the serving size, because everything else depends on it. Some tubs list one scoop, others two, and ready-to-drink cartons sometimes list half a bottle. If you will drink the whole bottle, double the numbers. Then look at total carbohydrate rather than sugar alone. A powder can be sugar-free and still carry 8 to 10 g of carbohydrate from maltodextrin, oat flour or added fiber blends, and it is total carbohydrate that most meal plans count.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, check added sugars, which US labels now list separately. Milk-based products contain some natural lactose, so a whey concentrate may show 2 or 3 g of sugar with zero added; that is a different thing from a powder sweetened with cane sugar. Fiber and sugar alcohols are listed under carbohydrate too. Fiber has little effect on glucose for most people, and sugar alcohols such as erythritol have a small one, but individual responses vary, which is why your own meter reading beats any label rule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then look at protein density, the share of calories that comes from protein. Multiply grams of protein by four and divide by total calories. A whey isolate with 25 g of protein and 110 calories lands near 90 percent; a mass gainer might land under 20 percent. For most people with diabetes, higher density is better because it means fewer stray carbohydrate and fat calories per gram of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, scan the ingredient list for the sweetener and anything you know does not agree with you. If your stomach objects to sugar alcohols or you would rather avoid artificial sweeteners, that decides more than any ranking. Our <a href="/best-low-carb-protein-powder/">low-carb protein powder guide</a> applies the same label test to a wider set of tubs if none of the picks above suits you.
          </p>

