        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sweeteners: Sucralose, Stevia, Sugar Alcohols or None
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every flavored low-sugar protein powder is sweetened with something, and the choice is one of the most common questions people with diabetes ask. The useful starting point is that the non-nutritive sweeteners approved in the US, including sucralose, acesulfame potassium, stevia extracts and monk fruit extract, do not raise blood glucose in the way sugar does. Used in the small amounts found in a scoop of protein, they let a shake taste sweet without spending carbohydrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The longer-term picture is less settled, and it is worth being honest about that. In 2023 the World Health Organization issued a conditional recommendation against using non-sugar sweeteners as a tool for weight control, based largely on observational links with weight gain and type 2 diabetes that may partly reflect who chooses diet products. The ADA's position is more practical: replacing sugar-sweetened foods and drinks with lower-sugar alternatives can help, while water and unsweetened options remain the better long-term habit. Neither body suggests that the sweetener in a daily protein shake is a meaningful danger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sugar alcohols deserve a separate mention. Erythritol, xylitol and similar ingredients have little effect on glucose but can cause bloating or diarrhea in larger amounts. A 2023 observational study also linked high blood erythritol levels to cardiovascular events; that is an association, not proof of harm, and it has not been settled by trials. If you have heart disease or would simply rather wait for better evidence, it is easy to choose a powder without erythritol.
          </p>

          <p>
            The simplest answer is unflavored. A single-ingredient whey or pea protein has no sweetener at all and can go into coffee, oats, yogurt or a smoothie sweetened with half a cup of berries. If you want flavor, stevia-sweetened isolates suit people avoiding artificial sweeteners, and sucralose-sweetened ones tend to taste closest to a classic shake. Pick the one you will drink consistently; that matters more than the sweetener debate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whey-before-meals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Whey Before Meals: What the Glucose Studies Actually Show
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most interesting reason to keep whey around is a small but consistent body of research on taking it shortly before a meal, sometimes called a whey preload. Whey is digested quickly, and its amino acids stimulate insulin and gut hormones including GLP-1, which can slow stomach emptying. The idea is that a little protein before the carbohydrate arrives blunts the rise that follows.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best-known early trial, published in Diabetologia in 2014 by Jakubowicz and colleagues, gave 15 adults with well-controlled type 2 diabetes either 50 g of whey in water or a placebo 30 minutes before a high-glycemic breakfast. Glucose over the next three hours was about 28 percent lower after whey, with higher insulin and GLP-1 responses. It was a striking result, but it used a large dose, a single test meal and a very small group.
          </p>

          <p>
            Later work tested more realistic amounts. A 2018 trial in 11 men with type 2 diabetes found that 15 g of whey with breakfast and lunch improved post-meal glucose and reduced appetite. In 2022, Smith and colleagues published a crossover trial in BMJ Open Diabetes Research and Care in which 18 adults with type 2 diabetes who were not using insulin drank a 15 g whey shot 10 minutes before breakfast, lunch and dinner for a week. Continuous glucose monitors showed roughly two more hours a day in the normal range and a modestly lower average glucose compared with placebo.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read those findings for what they are: short, small studies showing a real but modest effect on post-meal readings, mostly in people with type 2 diabetes who were not on insulin. They do not show long-term changes in HbA1c, and they do not make whey a substitute for medication. Because whey works partly by boosting insulin release, anyone on insulin or a sulfonylurea such as glipizide or glyburide should talk to their doctor before adding a pre-meal protein routine, since stacking effects on insulin could change how low your glucose goes. If you want to try it, a 15 g serving of plain whey in a small glass of water before your largest carbohydrate meal is the version closest to the research.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT THE EVIDENCE SUPPORTS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A modest, short-term drop in post-meal glucose, mostly in type 2 diabetes without insulin.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Trials are small and last days to weeks. Treat a whey preload as an experiment to run with your meter and your care team, not as a treatment, and check with your doctor first if you use insulin or a sulfonylurea.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="build-a-shake" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fiber, Fat and Building a Diabetes-Friendly Shake
          </h2>

          <p>
            A scoop of protein in water is a snack. On its own it is low in carbohydrate and calories, but it is also low in fiber, fat and micronutrients, which is why it rarely holds people as long as they expect. Turning it into something closer to a meal makes it more filling and usually flattens the glucose response of whatever carbohydrate you add.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fiber is the first addition. The ADA's guidance points people with diabetes toward at least the general population target of about 14 g of fiber per 1,000 calories, and most people fall well short. A tablespoon of chia or ground flax adds around 3 to 5 g, half a cup of raspberries about 4 g, and a handful of spinach disappears into almost any flavor. A little fat from nut butter, avocado or full-fat Greek yogurt slows digestion further and makes the shake taste like food rather than a supplement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mind the liquid. A cup of cow's milk carries about 12 g of carbohydrate, which is fine if you count it, and unsweetened almond or soy milk carries far less. Fruit is where shakes most often go wrong: a banana plus a cup of mango plus honey can easily push a smoothie past 60 g of carbohydrate. Half a cup of berries usually delivers flavor for a fraction of that. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">protein powder for smoothies guide</a> has more combinations that blend well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Meal replacement is a legitimate use when it is planned. Structured programs that swap one or two meals a day for portion-controlled shakes have research support for weight loss in type 2 diabetes, and the UK DiRECT trial used a medically supervised total diet replacement to put many participants' diabetes into remission. That is a supervised clinical program, not something to improvise with a tub from the supermarket, especially if you take glucose-lowering medication that may need adjusting as your intake drops.
          </p>

