        <div id="what-to-avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What to Avoid: Gainers, Smoothie Mixes and Sugary Bases
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mass gainers are the clearest product to skip. They exist to deliver calories, and most of those calories are fast-digesting carbohydrate. A full serving of a popular gainer such as Optimum Nutrition Serious Mass supplies about 1,250 calories, most of it from maltodextrin, per the label, which means well over 200 g of carbohydrate in a single shake. That is several meals' worth of carbohydrate for most people with diabetes. If you genuinely need to gain weight, a dietitian can help you do it with calorie-dense foods that will not send your glucose through the roof.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be wary of anything marketed as a meal shake, smoothie mix or recovery drink without checking the label. Some carry 20 to 40 g of sugar per serving, and many blender-shop smoothies labeled high-protein are built on juice, sherbet or frozen yogurt. Even some plant-based powders sold as clean or natural use cane sugar or coconut sugar and land at 5 to 10 g of sugar per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen is not a problem for glucose, but it is a problem for protein quality. It is an incomplete protein with no tryptophan and very little leucine, so it does little for muscle on its own. Our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen protein powder guide</a> covers when it is still worth taking. Products that advertise blood sugar support blends, cinnamon, berberine or bitter melon deserve particular care: some of these ingredients may lower glucose themselves and could interact with diabetes medication, and the doses inside a protein tub are rarely disclosed in a way you can check.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, avoid using a protein shake as a hypoglycemia snack or treatment. A low needs fast-acting carbohydrate, typically about 15 g from glucose tablets, juice or regular soda, followed by a recheck after 15 minutes. Protein and fat slow carbohydrate absorption, and the ADA advises against relying on high-protein foods to treat or prevent a low. Keep the shake for after you have recovered, if it helps hold you until the next meal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="talk-to-your-team" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Kidneys, Medications and When to Talk to Your Care Team
          </h2>

          <p>
            This guide is general information, not medical advice, and diabetes is a condition where the same food can be fine for one person and a problem for another. Before adding a daily protein supplement, it is worth a short conversation with your doctor, diabetes educator or a registered dietitian, particularly in the situations below.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If you have kidney disease</h3>

          <p>
            Diabetes is a leading cause of chronic kidney disease, and protein intake is one of the levers that matters once kidney function declines. The ADA's Standards of Care recommend that people with diabetes and non-dialysis kidney disease aim for about 0.8 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day, the standard recommended allowance, and note that higher intakes have been linked to faster loss of kidney function in this group. People on dialysis often need more protein, not less. If you have been told your eGFR is reduced or you have protein in your urine, get an individual target before adding a powder.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If you take insulin, a sulfonylurea or an SGLT2 inhibitor</h3>

          <p>
            Insulin and sulfonylureas lower glucose regardless of what you eat, so swapping a carbohydrate meal for a low-carb shake, or adding a pre-meal whey routine, can change your risk of lows; your prescriber may want to adjust doses. People taking an SGLT2 inhibitor such as empagliflozin or dapagliflozin should also check before cutting carbohydrate sharply, because very-low-carbohydrate eating with these drugs has been linked to a rare but serious form of ketoacidosis. If you also use a GLP-1 medication, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-glp-1/">protein powder for GLP-1 guide</a> covers small-volume options for low appetite.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pregnancy, allergies and other conditions</h3>

          <p>
            Gestational diabetes and pregnancy with pre-existing diabetes need individual nutrition plans, so let your obstetric team or dietitian sign off on any supplement. Milk-based powders are off the table with a milk allergy, and anyone with liver disease, gastroparesis or an eating disorder history should get tailored advice too. Our overview of whether <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder is safe</a> covers contaminants and third-party testing in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting sugar but not total carbohydrate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A powder can say zero sugar and still carry 8 to 10 g of carbohydrate from maltodextrin or flour fillers. Most meal plans count total carbohydrate, so read that line first, then check added sugars and fiber. Your meter, two hours after a shake, is the final word on how it works for you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a low with a protein shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein and fat slow carbohydrate absorption, so a shake is a poor way to bring up low blood glucose. Use about 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate such as glucose tablets or juice, recheck after 15 minutes, and save the shake for afterward if a meal is still a while away.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Building a fruit-and-juice smoothie around the powder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The protein is rarely the problem in a smoothie. A banana, mango, juice and honey can push one blender jug past 60 g of carbohydrate. Use unsweetened milk or water, half a cup of berries, and add chia, flax or nut butter for fullness instead of more fruit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding a pre-meal whey routine without telling your doctor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey works partly by boosting insulin release. That is modest and helpful for many people with type 2 diabetes, but on insulin or a sulfonylurea it could stack with your medication. Mention it at your next appointment, or call first if you have frequent lows.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring kidney function</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Diabetes is a leading cause of kidney disease, and protein targets change once kidney function declines. If your eGFR is reduced or your urine shows albumin, ask for an individual protein target before adding 25 to 50 g a day from shakes. People on dialysis usually need a different plan again.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best protein powder for diabetics?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people with diabetes, a whey protein isolate with zero or near-zero carbohydrate, such as Isopure Zero Carb, or a stevia-sweetened isolate if you avoid artificial sweeteners. An unflavored whey is the most flexible option for pre-meal protein and cooking. The best choice is the low-sugar powder you tolerate and will drink consistently, checked against your own meter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does protein powder raise blood sugar?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pure protein powders have a small direct effect on blood glucose compared with carbohydrate, because they contain little or no sugar or starch. Protein does stimulate some insulin, and flavored powders with maltodextrin or sugar can raise glucose more. Check total carbohydrate on the label and test your glucose two hours after a shake to see your own response.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey protein good for type 2 diabetes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Small short-term trials suggest that 15 to 50 g of whey taken shortly before a meal can lower the glucose rise after that meal, likely by boosting insulin and gut hormones such as GLP-1. The studies are small and mostly excluded insulin users, so treat it as a promising habit to discuss with your care team, not a treatment.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good diabetic protein shake to buy ready-made?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for about 20 to 30 g of protein with 1 to 2 g of sugar and single-digit carbohydrate per bottle. BOOST Glucose Control Max and Premier Protein both fit those numbers, per their labels. Meal-style diabetes shakes like Glucerna Hunger Smart carry less protein and more carbohydrate and fiber, so they suit a light meal rather than a protein top-up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are artificial sweeteners in protein powder safe for diabetics?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Approved non-nutritive sweeteners such as sucralose, stevia and monk fruit do not raise blood glucose like sugar does, and the amounts in a protein scoop are small. Long-term effects on weight and appetite are debated, and some people get digestive upset from sugar alcohols. If you prefer to avoid sweeteners, choose an unflavored powder.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a diabetic use protein shakes as meal replacements?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, when it is planned. A scoop in water is a snack; adding fiber, healthy fat and some berries makes it closer to a meal. Structured meal-replacement programs have research support for weight loss in type 2 diabetes, but if you take insulin or a sulfonylurea, ask your doctor first, because eating fewer carbohydrates may require medication changes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein should a diabetic eat per day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no separate protein target for diabetes with healthy kidneys; the ADA recommends individualizing intake around your usual pattern and goals, and the general adult baseline is the 0.8 g per kilogram RDA, with active and older adults often advised to eat more. With non-dialysis kidney disease the guidance is about 0.8 g per kilogram. A dietitian can set your exact number.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
