          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The best protein powder for diabetics is not a special formula. It is a plain, low-sugar protein with single-digit carbohydrate and a sweetener you are comfortable with, used to replace a sugary snack, strengthen a light breakfast or, if your care team agrees, taken as a small whey shot before your biggest carbohydrate meal. Zero-carb isolates, stevia-sweetened isolates and unflavored whey cover almost everyone, and the low-sugar ready-to-drink shakes cover the days you are away from a shaker.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Let your meter and your care team have the final word. Check your glucose two hours after a new shake, keep fast carbohydrate rather than protein for treating lows, and ask for an individual protein target if you have kidney disease, take insulin or a sulfonylurea, or are pregnant.
